### 8.2 Significance of the Work

The significance of this dissertation lies not only in the specific results obtained for the DAB converter but also in the **methodology** it advances. The multi‑tool, multi‑paradigm approach—combining theorem proving, reachability analysis, and statistical model checking within a contract‑based compositional framework—is applicable to a broad class of cyber‑physical systems beyond power electronics. As Beg (2017) demonstrated in his dissertation on formal verification of DC distribution networks, the synergy between power electronics and formal methods is a fertile area for research, and this dissertation extends that synergy from static verification to the domain of adaptive nonlinear control.

The mechanisation of the ISS proof in Lean 4 is a milestone for the power electronics community. While the formal methods community has long advocated for the use of proof assistants in safety‑critical system design, actual deployments in power electronics have been limited to static circuit theorems and PWM modulation properties. By mechanising a dynamic, adaptive control proof, we demonstrate that the technology is mature enough to handle the complexity of real‑world power converter control. This opens the door to a future in which control algorithms for safety‑critical converters (aerospace, medical, nuclear) are routinely subjected to machine‑checked verification before being committed to firmware.

The contract‑based framework provides a blueprint for how formal verification can be integrated into the existing industrial certification process. Rather than replacing the established workflow of simulation and testing, formal methods complement it by providing proof‑based evidence for those properties that are most critical for safety and most difficult to exhaustively test (e.g., stability under all possible load transients, thermal safety under all possible environmental conditions). The alignment with ISO 26262 and IEC 61508 ensures that the methodology is not merely academically elegant but industrially relevant.

